 - No wear detection. One shared password and one shared device key. Use HTTPS if you deploy.
 - Vibration strength cap (200/255) and 30 s cooldown are placeholders to confirm with a clinician and the motor datasheet.
 - Test EMI with the motor running (Task 1): it can disturb the I2C lines.
+
+## Review checks and file storage operation
+- Run `cd web && npm test` for isolated route tests (Node's built-in test runner; no added dependencies).
+- With the production server running, run `APP_PASSWORD=... DEVICE_KEY=... node test/http.test.mjs` for HTTP integration tests. These use synthetic data and delete the server's recorded history; use a disposable local instance.
+- All server processes must share the same working directory and persistent local filesystem. Writes use a directory lock and unique temporary files. If a process crashes with `web/data/.write-lock` present, stop every writer before removing that stale lock; waiting writes fail after two seconds.
+- Delete removes recorded hours and local backups, preserving settings and device status. Offline vest summaries can subsequently upload again; externally managed backups are outside this operation.
+- Network and calibration operations remain blocking. PWM is stopped around network and I2C operations; calibration starts and fault cues now share the existing 30-second cooldown with posture cues. A calibration sequence still includes its short acknowledgment pulses.
+- See `review/REVIEW.md`, `review/changes.diff`, and the recorded logs for evidence and remaining limitations. Next.js 14 dependencies still have audit findings; do not treat a passing build as deployment clearance.

